course: dictionary
lesson: 15960
title: Untied: a past action and a description
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Untied is the past form of the verb untie, which means to undo a knot, a tie or a rope. «Mum untied the parcel string.» Untied here is something that already happened. Untie is what you do; untied is what was done.
explain: Untied can also be an adjective, a word that describes a thing. «My shoelaces are untied.» Here untied tells you the laces are not tied. They are loose. Ask yourself: is the word showing an action that happened, or describing how something is now?
explain: Context tells you which job the word is doing. «She had untied the rope» shows an action finished in the past. «The rope hung down, untied» describes the rope. A quick check is to swap in "undone" or "loose". If the sentence still makes sense, untied is describing something.
fact: Untie means to undo a knot, a lace, a rope or a tie.
fact: Untied is the past form of untie: «She untied the knot» means she has already done it.
fact: As an adjective, untied means not tied: «untied shoelaces» are loose.
fact: Untied laces can make you trip, so check your shoes before you run.
fact: «Untied» can also describe someone set free: «The prisoner was untied» means the ropes were taken off.
exercise: «She untied the knot in her scarf.» What does untied mean? | Tied up very tightly, like a bow | Lost the scarf completely | *Undid the knot, the past of untie | Broke the scarf in two
exercise: «My shoelaces are untied, so I must tie them before I run.» What does untied describe? | *Not tied, so loose | Very tight and knotted | Brand new and clean | Happily running about
exercise: «Dad untied the boat.» Find the word that is the past form of untie. | *untied | Dad | rope | boat
test: «The prisoner was untied and set free.» What does untied mean here? | Sad and lonely in the cell | Tied again, very tightly | *Not tied any more, so free to move | Caught by the guards
test: Which sentence describes a rope that is not tied, rather than someone doing something? | Mia unties ropes every day | Mia untied the rope | Mia will untie the rope tomorrow | *The rope hung down, untied
exam: Jayden says «untied» is always a verb. Is he right? | Yes, it always shows an action | Yes, and it always means happy | *No, untied can also be an adjective, as in «untied laces» | No, untied is a noun, a thing you can hold
